### What is the Valid Anagram problem?

Given two strings, determine whether one is a rearrangement of the other. Two strings are valid anagrams if they have the same length and contain the same characters with the same frequencies. For example, `"listen"` and `"silent"` are valid anagrams.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ValidAnagram {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : t.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("listen", "silent")); // true
        System.out.println(isAnagram("hello", "world"));   // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### Why is the length check important?

The length check is both a performance optimisation and a correctness guarantee. If two strings have different lengths, they cannot possibly be anagrams. This O(1) check rejects obvious non-anagrams immediately.

More importantly, the length check establishes an invariant: if both strings have the same length and no count goes negative during the decrement pass, then all counts must end at zero. This means you do not need a final loop to verify all counts are zero.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class LengthInvariant {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) {
            System.out.println("Rejected: lengths " + s.length() + " vs " + t.length());
            return false;
        }
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : t.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        // No need to check all counts == 0 because lengths are equal
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("abc", "abcd")); // false
        System.out.println(isAnagram("abc", "cba"));  // true
    }
}
```

```text
Rejected: lengths 3 vs 4
false
true
```

### How does the early-negative exit work?

During the decrement pass, if any count drops below zero, the second string has more of that character than the first string provided. This means the strings cannot be anagrams, so you return false immediately without finishing the loop.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class EarlyNegativeExit {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;

        for (int i = 0; i < t.length(); i++) {
            int idx = t.charAt(i) - 'a';
            count[idx]--;
            if (count[idx] < 0) {
                System.out.println("Early exit at index " + i + ": '" + t.charAt(i) + "' excess");
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("aab", "bba")); // false
    }
}
```

```text
Early exit at index 2: 'a' excess
false
```

### How does the sort-and-compare alternative work?

The sort-and-compare approach converts both strings to char arrays, sorts them, and compares. If the sorted arrays are equal, the strings are anagrams. This is simpler to write but costs O(n log n) instead of O(n).

| Feature | Frequency count | Sort-and-compare |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(n) | O(n log n) |
| Space | O(1) for int[26] | O(n) for sorted copies |
| Early exit | Yes | No |
| Simplicity | Moderate | Very simple |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SortCompareAnagram {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;
        char[] a = s.toCharArray();
        char[] b = t.toCharArray();
        Arrays.sort(a);
        Arrays.sort(b);
        return Arrays.equals(a, b);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("debit", "bited")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
```

### How do you handle Unicode or mixed character sets?

When the input is not limited to lowercase ASCII, replace the fixed `int[26]` array with a `HashMap<Character, Integer>`. The logic is identical: increment for the first string, decrement for the second.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class UnicodeAnagram {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;
        Map<Character, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) count.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (char c : t.toCharArray()) {
            int val = count.merge(c, -1, Integer::sum);
            if (val < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("listen", "silent")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
```

### What about case-insensitive comparison?

If the comparison should ignore case, normalise both strings by lowercasing before running the frequency count. If spaces and punctuation should also be ignored, strip non-letter characters first.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CaseInsensitiveAnagram {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
        String a = s.toLowerCase().replaceAll("[^a-z]", "");
        String b = t.toLowerCase().replaceAll("[^a-z]", "");
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("Debit Card", "Bad Credit")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include empty strings, single characters, strings with all identical characters, and strings that differ in exactly one character.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class AnagramEdgeCases {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : t.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + isAnagram("", ""));       // true
        System.out.println("Single: " + isAnagram("a", "a"));    // true
        System.out.println("All same: " + isAnagram("aaa", "aaa")); // true
        System.out.println("Off by one: " + isAnagram("abc", "abd")); // false
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
Single: true
All same: true
Off by one: false
```

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, Rajesh screens pairs of account labels for shuffled duplicates. A label that is just a rearrangement of an existing one could be an attempt to create a disguised duplicate that evades a uniqueness rule. The system normalises labels by lowercasing and stripping non-letters, then runs the balanced-count check across many pairs. Suspicious matches are flagged for manual review by Priya.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class DuplicateLabelDetector {
    public static boolean isShuffledDuplicate(String existing, String incoming) {
        String a = existing.toLowerCase().replaceAll("[^a-z]", "");
        String b = incoming.toLowerCase().replaceAll("[^a-z]", "");
        if (a.equals(b)) return false; // exact match, not a shuffled duplicate
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true; // same letters, different arrangement
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isShuffledDuplicate("Rajesh Account", "Account Rajesh")); // true
        System.out.println(isShuffledDuplicate("Savings", "Savings"));               // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```
